<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Birsay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Birsay is a parish and village in the north-west of Mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The tidal island of the Brough of Birsay has remains of Pictish and Norse settlement, and in the 11th century Earl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thorfinn-the-mighty">Thorfinn the Mighty</a> made Birsay his seat and built a minster there, the first seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Orkney. The ruined Earl's Palace dates from the 16th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/birsay.jpg" alt="Birsay" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Comloquoy · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brough_of_Birsay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
